  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\n\nCurrently a push of moisture aloft is moving through the state\nin the form of high clouds. At the surface most of the area is\nor close to saturation, leading to sporadic fog. The denser fog\nis around Lake Sakakawea. Hi-res models have this area of fog \nslowly move east through the morning with upper level push of \nmoisture also moving east. A large trough has entered the \nWestern CONUS.\n\nIn the next 24 hours, the trough axis will move\nover the Rocky Mountains and create a weak Colorado Low. This\nlow looks to impact the state (mostly the western and northern \nhalf) Wednesday through Thursday. Today through Tuesday though, \nwill be much above normal with temperatures in the 50s and 60s \nwith the warmer southwest flow. Temperatures Wednesday will drop\nto the upper 30s northwest to 60 southeast. With these warm \ntemperatures the low pressure will create mostly rain showers \nuntil Wednesday night when it could switch to snow in the \nnorthwest. There is a less than 10 percent chance of 1 inch of \nsnow in the far northwest. QPF for the system looks to be around\n0.25 inches along the International Border, to almost zero near\nthe Interstate. As most low pressures do, Wednesday and \nThursday will be very breezy, especially on the southwest. We \nare looking at strong west winds on the backside of the low \n20-35 mph with gusts over 40mph. We may need a Wind Advisory in \nthe southwest Wednesday afternoon through the night. High \ntemperatures will remain in the 30s there after. \n\nDuring this same time a much stronger trough moves into the \nWest Coast. Friday morning this will create another Colorado Low\noff the Rocky Mountains. The ensembles continue to have decent \nagreement on the storm track, with the low passing across South \nDakota into Minnesota, though differences in relative strength \nof the system as well as precipitation type. As of now, the \nstorm would start Friday morning with a rain snow mix in the \ncentral and east, while snow will persist in the northwest. \nFriday evening as temperatures cool below freezing the chance \nfor all snow increases across all the west and most of the north\ncentral. By Saturday afternoon the whole system could move \nfully east of us. There is still uncertainties in the timing and\nspeed in the system however. \n\nNBM probabilities for 1 inch of snow are 20 to 30 percent \nacross the area, with pockets of 40 to 50 percent in the \nnorthwest and north central where cooler temperatures will be \nfound. For 2 inches we are currently looking at 10 to 20 percent\nall over and the same northwest and north central areas are at \n40 percent. Snow amounts are the biggest unknown with\nuncertainties in speed and surface temperatures. Some members \nof the ensembles have much more snow than others. Like the \nprevious low pressure system, west winds will increase on the \nbackside with gust up to 40mph in the James River Valley. The \nrest of the weekend, we will be under a shortwave ridge, and \nkeep temperatures in the 30s. Looking ahead to next week another\ntrough could pop off a low pressure system again.",
